<MarkSection path="/marks/chinese/yixing" prefix="cnyx" groups={YIXING_GROUPS} date="2026-11-27"
  title="Yixing Teapot Marks: Potters, Seals and Factory Marks | Heirloom"
  description="{n} Yixing potters, seals and marks, from Shi Dabin and Chen Mansheng to Gu Jingzhou and the Yixing factory, with the seal texts and what each says about date."
  h1="Yixing teapots and their seals"
  lede="A Yixing teapot is usually stamped with a seal under the base, and often smaller ones inside the lid and under the handle. The seals name a potter, a studio or a factory. Each entry gives the seals as they appear and what they do and do not prove."
  placeholder="Characters on the seal, or a name: 顧景舟, 孟臣, Shi Dabin" noun={['entry', 'entries']}
  app="Photograph the seals under the base, inside the lid and under the handle. The Advisor reads them and finds the potter or factory in this same reference.">
  <p>The famous names, Shi Dabin, Chen Mingyuan, Mengchen, appear on far more teapots than those potters could have made; most such seals are tributes or later copies. A seal is the potter’s claim, and the clay, the making and the style have to agree with it.</p>
